{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"wrongful-chemic-squeaker","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# tests/integration/test_s3_doctor_heldout.py::test_heal_runs_merge_under_write_lock is flaky\n\nObserved on CI run 31349549323 (Gerrit change 1577, patch set 4, ubuntu-latest / py3.11):\n\n    rebar._store.s3_doctor.S3DoctorConflict: could not bundle merged tip\n    b41953bf15019dfcfb0e5b7603868b5d6b998e4a for ref tickets: fatal: bad object HEAD\n\n## Why it looks like a flake, not a regression\n- The change under test (85fa) touches only `fsck_repair` / `fsck_restore` — the fsck restore\n  path. It does not touch `s3_doctor`, the store write path, or bundling.\n- The test PASSES locally on that exact rebased tree (run directly, alongside the other\n  reported failure; both green in 23s).\n- `fatal: bad object HEAD` while bundling a commit whose sha is already known is the signature\n  of a transient object-DB / HEAD-read race, not a logic error. The store code elsewhere\n  already carries explicit retries for exactly this class — see the\n  `_with_transient_head_retry` / object-DB `git add` retry wrappers in `_store/gitutil.py` and\n  `_store/event_append.py`, whose comments describe the same 'could not parse HEAD' transient\n  on CI runners.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Determine whether `s3_doctor`'s bundle step needs the same transient-HEAD retry the rest\n      of the store write path already has, or whether the TEST is racing its own fixture.\n- [ ] If it is a product gap, the retry is added and a test reproduces the transient\n      deterministically (inject the failure rather than hoping for the race).\n- [ ] If it is a test gap, the fixture is made deterministic.\n- [ ] Either way the test stops producing spurious `Verified -1` votes, which cost a real\n      review cycle to triage.\n\nRelated: bug ac76 (Flaky CI: git contention seam loses one concurrent writer) failed in the\nSAME CI run on a different Python cell. Both are store/git concurrency flakes surfacing\ntogether, so they may share a root cause in how the runners schedule these tiers.","id":"168e-8b84-087b-410e","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Flaky CI: s3_doctor heal test fails with 'fatal: bad object HEAD' when bundling the merged tip"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786375070977819001,"uuid":"0a7663e6-50cf-4b50-ac66-9b82b33df1e0"}